From {{inh|en|enm|cleyy}}, {{m|enm|cleyye|t=clayish; messy; unclean}}{{nb...|cleyi, clayie, cleyy, cleȝy, |otherforms=1}},<ref>{{R:MED Online|entry=cleiī|pos=adj|id=MED7934}}</ref> either:

* from {{inh|en|enm|clei}}, {{m|enm|cley|t=clay; clayey soil; clay-containing material used as mortar or plaster}}{{nb...|clai, claie, claigh, clay, claye, cleigh|otherforms=1}}<ref>{{R:MED Online|entry=clei|pos=n|id=MED7929}}</ref> + {{m|enm|-i|pos={{glossary|suffix}} forming {{glossary|adjective}}s}};<ref>{{R:MED Online|entry=-ī̆|pos=suf|id=MED21577}}</ref> {{m|enm||clei}}, {{m|enm||cley}} is derived from {{inh|en|ang|clǣġ|t=clay}}, ultimately from {{inh|en|ine-pro|*gleh₁y-}}, {{m|ine-pro|*gley-|t=to smear; to stick; glue; putty}}; or
* from {{inh|en|ang|clǣig|t=clayey}}, from {{m|ang|clǣġ|t=clay}} (see above) + {{m|ang|-iġ|pos=suffix forming adjectives}}.

The English word is equivalent to {{suffix|en|clay|ey|pos2=suffix forming adjectives with the sense ‘having the quality of’|id2=adjectival}}, with the ''-e-'' included to avoid the occurrence of ''-yy''.<ref>{{R:OED Online|pos=adj|id=34033|date=March 2021|nodot=1}}; {{R:Lexico|pos=adj}}</ref>

Sense 4 (“of the human body, as contrasted with the soul”) may allude to the [[Bible|biblical]] account of God creating man from earth; see [[w:Book of Genesis|Genesis]] 2:7 ({{w|King James Version}}; spelling modernized): “And the {{smallcaps|Lord}} God formed man of the dust of the ground, and breathed into his nostrils the breath of life, and man became a living soul.”<ref>{{R:King James Version|Genesis|2|7|page=79|column=2|passage=And the {{smallcaps|Lord}} God formed man of the duſt of the ground, & breathed into his noſtrils the breath of life, and man became a liuing soule.|nodot=1}}</ref>

